how ubisoft's rabbits become intertwined  with nintendo's mushroom kingdom  featuring mario and friends is  unimportant  you might not like rabbid style toilet  humor going in but there's an admirable  and wholehearted commitment in mario  plus rabbids kingdom battle that  triumphs in creating a magical game  world that is undeniably delightful and  within it  houses a deep challenging turn-based  tactical combat system  the invasion of dim-witted rabbids  brings out the sillier side of the  mushroom kingdom  but with some interesting extra  weirdness the humor is self-aware a  little more twisted and  conscious of the real world but there's  so much charm packed into every cartoony  crevice of mario plus rabbits  everything from the vibrant world the  incredibly expressive enemy animations  right down to the choco worthy text  descriptions of every item makes it hard  not to find something that makes you  smile  but the most significant and strangest  disruption to the lives of the mario  brothers  aside from their new creepy rabid  doppelganger allies  are guns explosives and the imperative  to use them  if you're familiar with the pharaaxis  reboot of the x-com series you know what  to expect  turn-based conflicts take place on a  gridded isometric battlefield where  projectile weapons  cover and flanking other major focus  however  kingdom battle twists that design  heavily by introducing abilities and  options  that encourage a more aggressive style  of fighting as well as situational  urgencies to create a faster paced more  exciting ebb and flow to battle  unlike other tactical games combatants  are allowed to perform movement attacks  and special abilities in a single turn  and in any order this means using a  special ability doesn't stop you from  attacking during that same turn  movement doesn't need to be your first  consideration and you don't need to  finish performing all actions with one  character before using another  characters can also use their movement  face to attack you can select enemies to  dash into on the way to an endpoint  you can also reach out of range  locations by moving into allies and  letting them give you a boost  a technique called team jump a variety  of special abilities across characters  allows you to perform actions like buff  weapon damage  scare enemies away from a location an  attack on enemy movement  a parallel to x-com's overwatch ability  altogether the flexibility of how these  abilities can be used and how they can  be combined  as well as the reward incentive for  exceptional performance  means that you're pushed to perform  elaborate team maneuvers every turn and  take  big satisfying risks finding chances to  perform complex action strings and  having them pay off  is incredibly exhilarating and it's in  these moments where kingdom battle is  its best  but these moments don't happen all the  time and they're not always worth the  risk  reaching these highs is all the more  sweeter since the combat design  constantly keeps you on your toes  most cover is easily destroyed for  example and a firing position you set  yourself up in at the end of your last  turn  can just as easily be vaporized by the  first enemy to act  leaving you completely exposed to  follow-up attacks you could be hit by a  weapon with a super effect  potentially setting you on fire and  causing you to run out of cover like a  maniac  or block your ability to use certain  actions enemy rabbids can be highly  aggressive and cunning especially in  later stages  you could also be the cause of your own  demise since friendly fire is a factor  and the more powerful characters like  peach and rabid mario specialize in  large  area of effect damage on top of that  you'll have to keep in mind the long  cooldowns on secondary weapons and  special abilities  as well as your party's health status a  close victory could mean that using the  same team members in the next fight puts  you at a disadvantage  kingdom battles combat will have you  agonizing over every facet of  every possible action during each of  your turns thankfully agonizing is made  easy because of the game's clean combat  interface  and the clear communication of pertinent  information at a glance you can see the  kinds of actions your team can still  perform in a turn  full in half cover is clearly marked and  the visual design of arenas makes it  obvious what is and isn't destructible  kingdom battle uses a numerical  percentage system to denote the chance  for an attack to make contact  but the only numbers you'll see are 0 50  and 100 meaning you know what the hit  outcome of your attack will be with  confidence  or you'll be completely certain that the  outcome will be uncertain  there's a larger variety of chance when  it comes to status effects but at no  point will you throw your hands up in  frustration because peach missed a 90  probability shotgun blast to a rabid  stupid face  the game also gives you the option to  completely respect character skill  points on a whim  and restart a skirmish at any time both  without punishment  so you might find yourself in a losing  battle due to a completely terrible team  composition  but kingdom battle encourages you to  experiment with different strategies by  restarting the mission  re-surveying the battlefield swapping  out team members and completely changing  their abilities in order to suit the  situation  the result is that despite its  difficulty kingdom battle gives off a  feeling of encouragement  much more than it does frustration in  its mechanical design alone  outside of the battles you run mario and  friends through the silly light-hearted  world of a mushroom kingdom that's been  invaded by butt-scratching rabbits and  spending time in this world is the  perfect dose of positivity to keep you  going after a tough fight it's the  seamlessness of this world which gives  kingdom battle its greatest feeling of  character  and place exploration and puzzle  segments blend into battle arenas and a  small checkpoint banner is the only  distinction between stages  each world feels like a huge tangible  location as a result  mildly challenging puzzles occasionally  gate the way forward but never halt the  pace of events and mainstays of mario  platforming games like red rings and  coin rooms  provide the odd distraction every once  in a while mario plus rabbit's kingdom  battle  exudes offbeat optimism that never  dissolves it's a consistent delight no  matter how challenging the road becomes  because kingdom battle's unique  turn-based tactics system is in every  way a pleasure to engage with  coupled with the annoyingly infectious  allure of rabbids  and the always delightful world of the  mushroom kingdom mario plus rabbids  kingdom battle  is an implausibly engrossing formula  that is positively challenging  and endlessly charming  you
 

